The hardest task in the program: M1 — persistent element identity (DraftElementID) from engine to document model. Repo /Users/x/Documents/edraft, branch rename/edraft, HEAD cdcc477 — tree is clean except eDraft.xcscheme (Xcode churn), .codedirector/mcp.log, and two repo-root PNGs; never stage or revert those, and you are the only agent working.
Why you: revisions, page locks and omissions-as-ids are all specced in docs/RFC-DRAFT-PRODUCTION.md to hang off DraftElementID (FORMAT §6.1: base-36 counter, never reused within a document). Today elements have no identity at all — IL-0071's omission record is an index span precisely because ids don't exist. You are building the foundation those features stand on. Read first, in this order: docs/RFC-DRAFT-FORMAT.md (§6.1), docs/RFC-DRAFT-PRODUCTION.md (§2, §6–§8, the Open Questions that touch identity), then how elements actually live today: packages/edraft/src/types.ts, apple/eDraftEngine/Sources/EDraftEngine/Model/Types.swift, and the document model in apple/EDraftCore (EditorState and friends).
Follow CLAUDE.md exactly: lock_draft → show me the Vibe Check → wait for my yes → lock_check + lock_activate → every change through run_locked → report. No commits, no co-author trailers.
Done means, at minimum: every script element carries a DraftElementID minted from the document's counter. Typing inside an element keeps its id. Splitting an element (Return) mints exactly one new id. Merging retires one. Pasted or imported content mints fresh ids — an id never moves between documents and is never reused within one. The .draft container (script.json) stores them; a plain .fountain has no spelling for ids, so those documents get session-stable ids minted on open — say so explicitly in the RFC's Open Questions style if the spec is silent. Both engines byte-identical, pinned in the conformance corpus. The app document model (EDraftCore) holds the ids through edits — this is what makes it M1 and not just an engine type.
Explicitly out of scope: revision sets UI, page-lock UI, omission-by-id migration, .draft save wiring in the apps, iOS. Those are later stages that consume your foundation; do not build them.
Proof bar: tests that fail before the change in both ports; npm test (currently 893+29), swift test for eDraftEngine (239+10), EDraftCore (350) and EDraftMacSurface (304, 6 skipped) all green; macOS Debug build with your own private DerivedData (CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO if the keychain stalls, and say so). The corpus may gain cases; every existing expectation stays unchanged. In your Vibe Check, present the identity model before any code: where the counter lives, who mints, who copies on split, what happens on undo — undo is where identity schemes go to die, so bring measurements, not vibes.